\subsection{URL Encoding for Binary Data}

When constructing tracker announce URLs, we must include the 20-byte info hash
and peer ID as query parameters. These are raw binary data---arbitrary byte
sequences that may contain null bytes, control characters, or any value from
0x00 to 0xFF. HTTP URLs require special characters to be percent-encoded, but
standard URL encoding libraries are often designed for text and may mishandle
raw binary data.

RFC 3986 defines which characters may appear unencoded in a URL. The
\emph{unreserved} characters are:
\begin{verbatim}
A-Z a-z 0-9 - _ . ~
\end{verbatim}
All other octets must be percent-encoded as \texttt{\%XX}, where \texttt{XX} is
the uppercase hexadecimal representation of the byte value.

For example, a null byte (0x00) becomes \texttt{\%00}, a space (0x20) becomes
\texttt{\%20}, and the byte 0xAB becomes \texttt{\%AB}. Since info hashes are
SHA1 digests, they contain essentially random bytes and will typically require
extensive encoding.

This is a common source of bugs in BitTorrent clients. If the encoding is
incorrect---using lowercase hex, failing to encode certain bytes, or
double-encoding already-encoded characters---the tracker will not recognize
the torrent and will return an error or empty peer list.

<<url encoding>>=
/// Returns true if the byte is an RFC 3986 unreserved character.
///
/// Unreserved characters can appear in URLs without percent-encoding:
/// - ALPHA: A-Z, a-z
/// - DIGIT: 0-9
/// - Hyphen, underscore, period, tilde: - _ . ~
///
/// All other bytes must be percent-encoded.
fn is_unreserved(byte: u8) -> bool {
    matches!(byte,
        b'A'..=b'Z' |
        b'a'..=b'z' |
        b'0'..=b'9' |
        b'-' | b'_' | b'.' | b'~'
    )
}

/// Percent-encode a 20-byte binary value for use in tracker URLs.
///
/// This function implements RFC 3986 percent-encoding, specifically designed
/// for the binary info_hash and peer_id parameters in BitTorrent tracker
/// announces.
///
/// # Arguments
///
/// * `bytes` - A 20-byte array (typically an info_hash or peer_id)
///
/// # Returns
///
/// A String where unreserved characters (A-Z, a-z, 0-9, -, _, ., ~) appear
/// as-is, and all other bytes are encoded as %XX with uppercase hex digits.
///
/// # Example
///
/// ```ignore
/// let hash = [0x12, 0x34, 0x56, /* ... */];
/// let encoded = url_encode(&hash);
/// // Result: "%124V..." (0x34 is '4', 0x56 is 'V', both unreserved)
/// ```
pub fn url_encode(bytes: &[u8; 20]) -> String {
    // Pre-allocate for worst case: all bytes encoded as %XX (3 chars each)
    let mut result = String::with_capacity(60);

    for &byte in bytes {
        if is_unreserved(byte) {
            result.push(byte as char);
        } else {
            // Format as %XX with uppercase hex
            result.push('%');
            result.push(to_hex_digit(byte >> 4));
            result.push(to_hex_digit(byte & 0x0F));
        }
    }

    result
}

/// Convert a nibble (0-15) to its uppercase hexadecimal character.
fn to_hex_digit(nibble: u8) -> char {
    match nibble {
        0..=9 => (b'0' + nibble) as char,
        10..=15 => (b'A' + nibble - 10) as char,
        _ => unreachable!("nibble must be 0-15"),
    }
}
@

The implementation iterates over each byte and either passes it through
unchanged (if unreserved) or encodes it as a three-character percent-escape
sequence. We use uppercase hexadecimal as required by the de facto BitTorrent
specification---some trackers are case-sensitive about hex encoding.

Note that we pre-allocate the worst-case capacity (60 characters for 20 bytes,
each becoming \texttt{\%XX}). In practice, SHA1 hashes will have roughly 5--6
unreserved bytes on average, so the typical output is around 52--54 characters.

\subsection{URL Encoding Tests}

We verify the URL encoder handles all the edge cases that commonly trip up
BitTorrent implementations.

<<url encoding tests>>=
#[test]
fn url_encode_all_zeros() {
    // 20 null bytes should produce 20 occurrences of %00
    let zeros = [0u8; 20];
    let encoded = url_encode(&zeros);
    assert_eq!(encoded, "%00".repeat(20));
    assert_eq!(encoded.len(), 60); // 20 * 3 characters
}

#[test]
fn url_encode_unreserved_chars_pass_through() {
    // Test that unreserved characters are not encoded
    // Using ASCII values that spell out unreserved chars
    let input: [u8; 20] = [
        b'A', b'Z', b'a', b'z', b'0', b'9', b'-', b'_', b'.', b'~',
        b'B', b'Y', b'b', b'y', b'1', b'8', b'-', b'_', b'.', b'~',
    ];
    let encoded = url_encode(&input);
    assert_eq!(encoded, "AZaz09-_.~BYby18-_.~");
    assert_eq!(encoded.len(), 20); // No percent-encoding needed
}

#[test]
fn url_encode_known_sha1_hash() {
    // SHA1("hello") = aaf4c61ddcc5e8a2dabede5b54ca7aa4e7379202
    // We use a subset that includes both encodable and unreserved bytes
    let hash: [u8; 20] = [
        0xAA, 0xF4, 0xC6, 0x1D, 0xDC, 0xC5, 0xE8, 0xA2, 0xDA, 0xBE,
        0xDE, 0x5B, 0x54, 0xCA, 0x7A, 0xA4, 0xE7, 0x37, 0x92, 0x02,
    ];
    let encoded = url_encode(&hash);
    // 0x5B = '[' (must encode), 0x54 = 'T' (unreserved), 0x7A = 'z' (unreserved)
    // 0x37 = '7' (unreserved), 0x02 = control char (must encode)
    assert!(encoded.contains("T"));  // 0x54 should not be encoded
    assert!(encoded.contains("z"));  // 0x7A should not be encoded
    assert!(encoded.contains("7"));  // 0x37 should not be encoded
    assert!(encoded.contains("%5B")); // 0x5B '[' must be encoded
    assert!(encoded.contains("%02")); // 0x02 must be encoded
    // Verify uppercase hex
    assert!(!encoded.contains("%5b")); // Must be uppercase
    assert!(!encoded.contains("%aa")); // Must be uppercase
}

#[test]
fn url_encode_special_characters() {
    // Test that reserved and special characters are properly encoded
    // These bytes correspond to characters that have special meaning in URLs
    let input: [u8; 20] = [
        b'/', b'?', b'#', b'[', b']', b'@', b'!', b'$', b'&', b'\'',
        b'(', b')', b'*', b'+', b',', b';', b'=', b' ', b'%', b':',
    ];
    let encoded = url_encode(&input);
    // All of these must be percent-encoded
    assert!(encoded.contains("%2F")); // '/'
    assert!(encoded.contains("%3F")); // '?'
    assert!(encoded.contains("%23")); // '#'
    assert!(encoded.contains("%5B")); // '['
    assert!(encoded.contains("%5D")); // ']'
    assert!(encoded.contains("%40")); // '@'
    assert!(encoded.contains("%20")); // space
    assert!(encoded.contains("%25")); // '%'
    // Verify none pass through unencoded
    assert!(!encoded.contains('/'));
    assert!(!encoded.contains('?'));
    assert!(!encoded.contains('#'));
    assert!(!encoded.contains(' '));
}

#[test]
fn url_encode_high_bytes() {
    // Test bytes >= 0x80 (non-ASCII range)
    let input: [u8; 20] = [
        0x80, 0x81, 0x90, 0xA0, 0xB0, 0xC0, 0xD0, 0xE0, 0xF0, 0xFF,
        0x82, 0x83, 0x91, 0xA1, 0xB1, 0xC1, 0xD1, 0xE1, 0xF1, 0xFE,
    ];
    let encoded = url_encode(&input);
    // All high bytes must be encoded
    assert!(encoded.contains("%80"));
    assert!(encoded.contains("%FF"));
    assert!(encoded.contains("%FE"));
    // Verify uppercase
    assert!(!encoded.contains("%ff"));
    assert!(!encoded.contains("%fe"));
    // All 20 bytes should be encoded as 3 chars each
    assert_eq!(encoded.len(), 60);
}

#[test]
fn url_encode_round_trip() {
    // Verify that percent-decoding the output yields the original bytes
    let original: [u8; 20] = [
        0x12, 0x34, 0x56, 0x78, 0x9A, 0xBC, 0xDE, 0xF0, 0x00, 0xFF,
        b'A', b'z', b'0', b'-', b'_', b'.', b'~', 0x01, 0x7F, 0x80,
    ];
    let encoded = url_encode(&original);
    let decoded = percent_decode(&encoded);
    assert_eq!(decoded.as_slice(), original.as_slice());
}

/// Helper function to decode percent-encoded strings for testing.
/// This is only used in tests, not in the main library.
fn percent_decode(s: &str) -> Vec<u8> {
    let mut result = Vec::with_capacity(s.len());
    let mut chars = s.chars();
    while let Some(c) = chars.next() {
        if c == '%' {
            let hex: String = chars.by_ref().take(2).collect();
            if let Ok(byte) = u8::from_str_radix(&hex, 16) {
                result.push(byte);
            }
        } else {
            result.push(c as u8);
        }
    }
    result
}
@

These tests cover the key scenarios:
\begin{enumerate}
\item \textbf{All zeros}: The worst case for encoding, producing the longest
  output. This catches bugs where null bytes are mishandled.
\item \textbf{Unreserved characters}: Verifies that the 66 unreserved ASCII
  characters pass through without encoding.
\item \textbf{Known SHA1 hash}: Uses a real hash value to test a realistic
  mixture of encodable and unreserved bytes.
\item \textbf{Special characters}: Tests URL-significant characters that must
  never appear unencoded.
\item \textbf{High bytes}: Verifies correct handling of bytes 0x80--0xFF, which
  are outside the ASCII range.
\item \textbf{Round-trip}: Decodes the encoded output and verifies it matches
  the original bytes, ensuring no data loss.
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{Peer Identification}

Every BitTorrent client announces itself to trackers and other peers using a
20-byte \emph{peer ID}. This identifier serves two purposes: it uniquely
identifies this particular client session (allowing peers to recognize
reconnections), and it indicates which software the client is running (useful
for debugging interoperability issues in swarms).

BEP 20 defines conventions for encoding client information into the peer ID.
The most widely adopted is the \textbf{Azureus-style} format:

\begin{verbatim}
-XX1234-xxxxxxxxxxxx
\end{verbatim}

The structure is:
\begin{description}
\item[Byte 0]: Hyphen (\texttt{-})
\item[Bytes 1--2]: Two-character client identifier (e.g., \texttt{AZ} for
  Azureus, \texttt{TR} for Transmission, \texttt{qB} for qBittorrent)
\item[Bytes 3--6]: Four ASCII digits encoding the version number (e.g.,
  \texttt{0001} for version 0.0.01)
\item[Byte 7]: Hyphen (\texttt{-})
\item[Bytes 8--19]: Twelve random bytes ensuring uniqueness across sessions
\end{description}

For our Literate Torrent client, we use \texttt{LT} as the client identifier
and \texttt{0001} as the initial version. The random suffix ensures that even
if multiple instances run on the same machine, they will have distinct peer
IDs and can participate in the same swarm without confusion.

The peer ID is generated once when the client starts and reused for all
tracker announces and peer connections during that session. Changing the peer
ID mid-session would confuse trackers and peers, potentially causing them to
count us multiple times or fail to recognize our announce events.

<<peer id generation>>=
/// The Azureus-style client identifier prefix for Literate Torrent.
///
/// Format: "-LT0001-" where:
/// - LT = Literate Torrent (our client identifier)
/// - 0001 = version 0.0.01
///
/// This prefix is followed by 12 random bytes to form the complete 20-byte peer_id.
pub const PEER_ID_PREFIX: &[u8; 8] = b"-LT0001-";

/// Generate a unique 20-byte peer ID for this client session.
///
/// The peer ID follows the Azureus-style convention defined in BEP 20:
/// - First 8 bytes: "-LT0001-" (client identifier and version)
/// - Last 12 bytes: cryptographically random data
///
/// This function should be called once at client startup. The returned peer ID
/// should be reused for all tracker announces and peer handshakes during the
/// session.
///
/// # Returns
///
/// A 20-byte array suitable for use in tracker announces and peer handshakes.
///
/// # Example
///
/// ```ignore
/// let peer_id = generate_peer_id();
/// assert_eq!(&peer_id[0..8], b"-LT0001-");
/// assert_eq!(peer_id.len(), 20);
/// ```
pub fn generate_peer_id() -> [u8; 20] {
    use rand::RngCore;

    let mut peer_id = [0u8; 20];

    // Copy the client identifier prefix
    peer_id[0..8].copy_from_slice(PEER_ID_PREFIX);

    // Fill remaining 12 bytes with random data
    rand::thread_rng().fill_bytes(&mut peer_id[8..]);

    peer_id
}
@

The implementation is straightforward: we start with our fixed 8-byte prefix
and fill the remaining 12 bytes with random data from the operating system's
cryptographically secure random number generator (via the [[rand]] crate's
default thread-local RNG).

Note that we use [[fill_bytes]] rather than generating individual random bytes
in a loop. This is both more efficient and clearer in intent---we want 12
random bytes, not 12 random numbers that happen to be bytes.

