\subsection{Tracker Response Parsing}

After sending an announce request, the tracker returns a bencoded dictionary.
This response contains either an error message or the information we need:
the list of peers and when to announce again. BEP 3 defines the response
format precisely, and we must handle both success and failure cases.

\subsubsection{Response Format}

A successful tracker response is a bencoded dictionary containing:

\begin{description}
\item[interval] (required) The number of seconds the client should wait
  between regular announce requests. Trackers use this to control load---a
  popular torrent might have a 30-minute interval, while a less-trafficked
  one might allow 5-minute intervals.

\item[min interval] (optional) The minimum interval between announces. If
  present, the client must not announce more frequently than this, even
  when events occur. Our client respects this by waiting at least this
  long between any two announces.

\item[peers] (required) The list of peers. This can be in two formats:
  \begin{itemize}
  \item \emph{Compact format} (BEP 23): A byte string where each peer is
    6 bytes---4 bytes for the IPv4 address and 2 bytes for the port, both
    in network byte order (big-endian). For $n$ peers, the string is
    $6n$ bytes.
  \item \emph{Dictionary format}: A list of dictionaries, each containing
    ``peer id'', ``ip'', and ``port'' keys. This format is deprecated and
    wastes bandwidth, but some older trackers still use it.
  \end{itemize}

\item[complete] (optional) The number of seeders (peers with the complete
  file). Useful for display purposes and deciding whether to start a download.

\item[incomplete] (optional) The number of leechers (peers still downloading).
  Together with ``complete'', this indicates swarm health.

\item[tracker id] (optional) An opaque string the client should echo back
  in subsequent announces. We don't use this in our implementation, but
  we document it for completeness.
\end{description}

A failure response contains a single key:

\begin{description}
\item[failure reason] A human-readable string explaining why the tracker
  rejected the request. Common reasons include ``torrent not registered''
  and ``invalid info\_hash''.
\end{description}

When ``failure reason'' is present, no other keys are required---the tracker
is telling us the request failed and there's no useful information to extract.

\subsubsection{The Response Structure}

We define a struct to hold the parsed response. The [[peers]] field stores
raw bytes because peer parsing is handled by a separate task (task-0070).
This separation keeps the response parsing focused on the tracker protocol
while deferring the peer address extraction.

<<tracker response struct>>=
/// A successful response from a BitTorrent tracker.
///
/// This struct contains the information returned by the tracker after a
/// successful announce request. The peer list is stored as raw bytes to
/// defer parsing to a separate component.
///
/// # Fields
///
/// See BEP 3 for the complete specification of tracker responses.
///
/// # Example
///
/// ```ignore
/// let response = parse_tracker_response(&body)?;
/// println!("Next announce in {} seconds", response.interval);
/// println!("Received {} bytes of peer data", response.peers.len());
/// ```
#[derive(Debug, Clone, PartialEq, Eq)]
pub struct TrackerResponse {
    /// Seconds until the client should send another regular announce.
    ///
    /// The client must wait at least this long between periodic announces.
    /// Events (started, stopped, completed) may trigger immediate announces,
    /// but regular re-announces should respect this interval.
    pub interval: u64,

    /// Minimum seconds between any two announces (optional).
    ///
    /// If present, the client must not announce more frequently than this,
    /// even when events occur. This takes precedence over `interval` as the
    /// absolute minimum wait time.
    pub min_interval: Option<u64>,

    /// Raw peer data from the tracker.
    ///
    /// This is stored as raw bytes because the format depends on whether
    /// the tracker returned compact (BEP 23) or dictionary format. The
    /// peer parsing logic (task-0070) handles interpretation.
    ///
    /// For compact format: 6 bytes per peer (4-byte IPv4 + 2-byte port).
    /// For dictionary format: this contains the bencoded list.
    pub peers: Vec<u8>,

    /// Number of seeders in the swarm (optional).
    ///
    /// Seeders are peers that have the complete file. This information
    /// is useful for display and for deciding whether to start a download
    /// (a torrent with zero seeders may never complete).
    pub complete: Option<u64>,

    /// Number of leechers in the swarm (optional).
    ///
    /// Leechers are peers still downloading. Together with `complete`,
    /// this indicates swarm activity and health.
    pub incomplete: Option<u64>,
}
@

The struct uses [[Option]] for fields that the tracker may omit. The [[peers]]
field is always present in a successful response, so it's not optional---if
there are no peers, the tracker returns an empty byte string or list.

\subsubsection{Parsing the Response}

The [[parse_tracker_response]] function takes raw bytes (the HTTP response body)
and returns either a [[TrackerResponse]] or an error. The parsing logic follows
this order:

\begin{enumerate}
\item Decode the bytes as bencode. If this fails, return [[TrackerInvalidResponse]].
\item Check for a ``failure reason'' key. If present, return [[TrackerErrorResponse]].
\item Extract the required ``interval'' field. If missing or not an integer,
  return [[TrackerInvalidResponse]].
\item Extract the ``peers'' field. Handle both compact (byte string) and
  dictionary (list) formats.
\item Extract optional fields: ``min interval'', ``complete'', ``incomplete''.
\item Construct and return the [[TrackerResponse]].
\end{enumerate}

<<tracker response parsing>>=
use crate::bencode::{decode, BencodeValue};

/// Parse a bencoded tracker response into a structured `TrackerResponse`.
///
/// This function handles the complete parsing of a tracker's HTTP response body,
/// including error detection and field extraction. It supports both compact (BEP 23)
/// and dictionary peer formats.
///
/// # Arguments
///
/// * `data` - The raw bytes of the tracker's HTTP response body
///
/// # Returns
///
/// On success, returns a `TrackerResponse` containing the interval, peers, and
/// optional metadata.
///
/// # Errors
///
/// Returns [`TrackerError::TrackerInvalidResponse`] if:
/// - The data is not valid bencode
/// - The response is not a dictionary
/// - The required `interval` field is missing or not an integer
/// - The required `peers` field is missing
/// - The `peers` field is neither a byte string nor a list
///
/// Returns [`TrackerError::TrackerErrorResponse`] if the response contains a
/// `failure reason` key, with the failure message as the error payload.
///
/// # Example
///
/// ```ignore
/// let body = announce_http(&url).await?;
/// let response = parse_tracker_response(&body)?;
/// println!("Announce again in {} seconds", response.interval);
/// ```
pub fn parse_tracker_response(data: &[u8]) -> Result<TrackerResponse, TrackerError> {
    // Step 1: Decode as bencode
    let value = decode(data).map_err(|e| {
        TrackerError::TrackerInvalidResponse(format!("invalid bencode: {}", e))
    })?;

    // Step 2: Ensure it's a dictionary
    let dict = match value {
        BencodeValue::Dict(d) => d,
        _ => {
            return Err(TrackerError::TrackerInvalidResponse(
                "response is not a dictionary".to_string(),
            ))
        }
    };

    // Step 3: Check for failure reason (BEP 3 error response)
    if let Some(reason) = dict.get(b"failure reason".as_slice()) {
        let message = match reason {
            BencodeValue::Bytes(b) => {
                String::from_utf8_lossy(b).into_owned()
            }
            _ => "unknown error".to_string(),
        };
        return Err(TrackerError::TrackerErrorResponse(message));
    }

    // Step 4: Extract required "interval" field
    let interval = dict
        .get(b"interval".as_slice())
        .ok_or_else(|| {
            TrackerError::TrackerInvalidResponse("missing 'interval' field".to_string())
        })?;

    let interval = match interval {
        BencodeValue::Integer(n) if *n >= 0 => *n as u64,
        BencodeValue::Integer(_) => {
            return Err(TrackerError::TrackerInvalidResponse(
                "'interval' is negative".to_string(),
            ))
        }
        _ => {
            return Err(TrackerError::TrackerInvalidResponse(
                "'interval' is not an integer".to_string(),
            ))
        }
    };

    // Step 5: Extract required "peers" field
    let peers_value = dict.get(b"peers".as_slice()).ok_or_else(|| {
        TrackerError::TrackerInvalidResponse("missing 'peers' field".to_string())
    })?;

    // Peers can be either compact (byte string) or dictionary format (list)
    let peers = match peers_value {
        BencodeValue::Bytes(b) => b.clone(),
        BencodeValue::List(_) => {
            // For dictionary format, we store the re-encoded bencode
            // The peer parser (task-0070) will handle this
            crate::bencode::encode(peers_value)
        }
        _ => {
            return Err(TrackerError::TrackerInvalidResponse(
                "'peers' is neither a byte string nor a list".to_string(),
            ))
        }
    };

    // Step 6: Extract optional "min interval" field
    let min_interval = dict.get(b"min interval".as_slice()).and_then(|v| {
        match v {
            BencodeValue::Integer(n) if *n >= 0 => Some(*n as u64),
            _ => None,
        }
    });

    // Step 7: Extract optional "complete" (seeders) field
    let complete = dict.get(b"complete".as_slice()).and_then(|v| {
        match v {
            BencodeValue::Integer(n) if *n >= 0 => Some(*n as u64),
            _ => None,
        }
    });

    // Step 8: Extract optional "incomplete" (leechers) field
    let incomplete = dict.get(b"incomplete".as_slice()).and_then(|v| {
        match v {
            BencodeValue::Integer(n) if *n >= 0 => Some(*n as u64),
            _ => None,
        }
    });

    Ok(TrackerResponse {
        interval,
        min_interval,
        peers,
        complete,
        incomplete,
    })
}
@

\subsection{Compact Peer List Parsing (BEP-23)}

Once we have the raw peer data from the tracker response, we need to parse it
into usable socket addresses. BEP-23 defines the \emph{compact} peer format,
which is far more efficient than the older dictionary format and is supported
by virtually all modern trackers.

In compact format, each peer is represented as exactly 6 bytes:

\begin{description}
\item[Bytes 0--3] The IPv4 address in network byte order (big-endian). The first
  byte is the most significant octet of the address. For example, the address
  192.168.1.1 would be encoded as the bytes \texttt{0xC0 0xA8 0x01 0x01}.

\item[Bytes 4--5] The TCP port number in network byte order (big-endian). For
  example, the standard BitTorrent port 6881 (0x1AE1) would be encoded as
  \texttt{0x1A 0xE1}.
\end{description}

The total peer data length must be divisible by 6. If the tracker returns
compact peer data with a length not divisible by 6, the response is malformed
and we reject it. An empty peer list (0 bytes) is valid and simply means no
peers are currently available---perhaps the torrent is new or all peers have
disconnected.

This format is called ``compact'' because it uses only 6 bytes per peer,
compared to the dictionary format which requires dozens of bytes per peer
for the bencoded dictionary structure, key names, and quoted string values.
For a response with 50 peers, compact format uses 300 bytes while dictionary
format might use 2000+ bytes.

<<compact peer parsing>>=
use std::net::{Ipv4Addr, SocketAddr, SocketAddrV4};

/// The number of bytes used to represent each peer in compact format.
///
/// BEP-23 specifies: 4 bytes for IPv4 address + 2 bytes for port = 6 bytes.
pub const COMPACT_PEER_SIZE: usize = 6;

/// Parse a compact peer list into socket addresses.
///
/// BEP-23 defines the compact peer format where each peer is exactly 6 bytes:
/// - Bytes 0-3: IPv4 address (big-endian / network byte order)
/// - Bytes 4-5: Port number (big-endian / network byte order)
///
/// # Arguments
///
/// * `data` - Raw peer data from the tracker's `peers` field
///
/// # Returns
///
/// On success, returns a vector of socket addresses. The vector may be empty
/// if no peers were provided (valid: the swarm may be empty).
///
/// # Errors
///
/// Returns [`TrackerError::TrackerInvalidResponse`] if the data length is not
/// divisible by 6, indicating a malformed compact peer list.
///
/// # Example
///
/// ```ignore
/// // Parse tracker response, then extract peers
/// let response = parse_tracker_response(&body)?;
/// let peers = parse_compact_peers(&response.peers)?;
/// for peer in peers {
///     println!("Peer: {}", peer);
/// }
/// ```
pub fn parse_compact_peers(data: &[u8]) -> Result<Vec<SocketAddr>, TrackerError> {
    // Validate length is divisible by 6
    if data.len() % COMPACT_PEER_SIZE != 0 {
        return Err(TrackerError::TrackerInvalidResponse(format!(
            "compact peer data length {} is not divisible by 6",
            data.len()
        )));
    }

    // Empty peer list is valid (no peers available)
    if data.is_empty() {
        return Ok(Vec::new());
    }

    // Parse each 6-byte chunk into a SocketAddr
    let peer_count = data.len() / COMPACT_PEER_SIZE;
    let mut peers = Vec::with_capacity(peer_count);

    for chunk in data.chunks_exact(COMPACT_PEER_SIZE) {
        // Extract IPv4 address (bytes 0-3, big-endian)
        let ip = Ipv4Addr::new(chunk[0], chunk[1], chunk[2], chunk[3]);

        // Extract port (bytes 4-5, big-endian)
        let port = u16::from_be_bytes([chunk[4], chunk[5]]);

        peers.push(SocketAddr::V4(SocketAddrV4::new(ip, port)));
    }

    Ok(peers)
}
@

The implementation is straightforward thanks to Rust's standard library:

\begin{itemize}
\item [[Ipv4Addr::new]] takes four octets in the natural order (most significant
  first), which matches the network byte order in the compact format.

\item [[u16::from_be_bytes]] explicitly decodes the port from big-endian bytes,
  making the byte ordering clear in the code. This is more readable than
  manual bit shifting (\texttt{(chunk[4] as u16) @<@< 8 | chunk[5] as u16}).

\item [[chunks_exact]] ensures we process exactly 6 bytes at a time and would
  panic if the slice length wasn't divisible by 6---but we've already validated
  that, so this is safe.

\item We pre-allocate the vector capacity since we know exactly how many peers
  we'll parse. This avoids reallocations during parsing.
\end{itemize}

The parsing function is deliberately defensive. We check for failure reasons
before extracting other fields because a failure response may not contain
valid data for those fields. We also validate that integers are non-negative
before casting to [[u64]], since negative intervals or peer counts don't make
sense.

For the peers field, we handle both formats:
\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Compact format}: The bytes are stored directly. This is the
  common case with modern trackers.
\item \textbf{Dictionary format}: We re-encode the list to bytes so the peer
  parser can decode it uniformly. This adds a small overhead for deprecated
  tracker responses, but keeps the peer parsing logic clean.
\end{itemize}

